                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="720-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>AKG Acoustics Perception 170</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Pencil Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Perception 170 is a minor revision to the company&#8217;s discontinued Perception 150. Both are half-inch, cardioid FET condenser mics with transformerless output circuits. The only difference in the published descriptions or specs is the preattenuation pad, which in the model 170 is -20dB.</p><p>The mic&#8217;s published frequency graph shows a relatively flat response from 70Hz - 7000Hz, dipping 1dB from 200-300Hz and 2dB around 5kHz, then spiking to +6dB at 10kHz. This graph is identical to that of the Perception 150.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/Perception-SDC-head-comparison.jpg" width="200" alt="Perception 150/Perception 170 Capsule Head Comparison" class="thumb shaft" />Note the differences in capsule vent size and shape of the Perception 150 (left) and Perception 170 (right). Although subtle, these differences could have an effect on the high-frequency response of the two microphones, despite the identical response graphs.</p><p>The mic ships in a cardboard box with a storage pouch and a mic clip (AKG p/n SA 45).</p><p>The Perception 170, like the rest of the Perception series, is manufactured in China to AKG&#8217;s specs and standards.</p><blockquote><p><cite>SoundOnSound, 2009</cite></p><p>The presence peak translates to an open airiness at the top end, but because the peak is right up at 10kHz it doesn&#8217;t make the high end seem harsh or honky&#8230; [T]he mic fared well on the acoustic guitar test, combining a confident low end with a nice but subtle sense of airy detail that was more pronounced than with the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/Perception-420">[Perception] 420</a>, but was still not overdone. It handles hand percussion equally capably.</p></blockquote><p>The Perception 170 was replaced in AKG&#8217;s product line by the P170.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/Perception-170">AKG Acoustics Perception-170</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2008.</p>
